        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which circuit breaker finder suits most homeowners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For mapping a home panel, a plug-in finder with automatic sensitivity and outlet testing, such as the Klein ET310, is the best fit. If you will adjust a dial, the Extech CB10 or Sperry CS61200 costs less. Buy a tracer like the Klein ET450 only if you need to work on dead or hidden wiring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are breaker finders accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In tidy residential panels, good finders usually identify a single breaker. In crowded panels with tandem breakers, multiwire circuits or tangled wiring, they often indicate two or three candidates. Lower the sensitivity, keep the receiver orientation consistent, and confirm every result by switching the breaker off. Automatic receivers usually need less fiddling than manual ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a breaker finder find a dead circuit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plug-in breaker finders need a live outlet to inject their signal, so they cannot find a breaker for a circuit that is already off or broken. Circuit tracers such as the Klein ET450 or Fluke 2042 can connect to de-energized wiring with clip leads and follow it. Plug-in kits also cannot help with a circuit whose only outlets are dead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my finder beep on more than one breaker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The signal can couple onto adjacent breakers and conductors in a crowded panel, the sensitivity may be set too high, or the circuit may be a multiwire branch circuit sharing a neutral. Reduce sensitivity, recalibrate an automatic receiver, and confirm by switching off each candidate in turn. Unplugging electronics on the circuit can also help.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do breaker finders work on subpanels?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Plug the transmitter into a circuit fed from the subpanel and sweep the subpanel breakers. The finder indicates the branch breaker there, not the feeder breaker in the main panel. Map each panel separately, and label the main panel feeder with the subpanel's location. The same method works for detached garage panels.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is using a breaker finder safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plug-in finders are low risk because they plug into a normal outlet and work through the closed panel cover. The danger comes afterward, if someone assumes a circuit is dead because the finder pointed to a breaker. Always verify with a working voltage tester before touching any conductor. Leave the panel cover on throughout the job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a breaker finder map 240-volt circuits?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most plug-in finders are rated for 120-volt outlets only and cannot be plugged into dryer, range or other 240-volt receptacles. Identify those double-pole breakers by turning the appliance controls off, switching off the suspected breaker and checking that the appliance will not start. A clamp meter can also confirm which breaker carries an appliance's current.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
